<Layout title="The dhurrie: five weeks on the loom — AASRA Journal" description="What handweaving a cotton dhurrie actually asks of the hands that do it.">
  <div class="page-title-block">
    <div class="kicker">Journal · Craft</div>
    <h1>The dhurrie: five weeks on the loom</h1>
    <p class="page-sub">From cotton thread to flat-woven rug.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:760px;">
      <img src="/images/campaign/story-artisan.webp" alt="Hands working a wooden handloom" loading="eager" width="1600" height="1000" style="width:100%; aspect-ratio:16/10; object-fit:cover; display:block; margin-bottom:2.5rem;" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>A dhurrie announces its honesty from across the room. No pile, no print — just two sets of cotton threads, crossed over months of decisions. It is the oldest furniture in South Asian homes, and the least changed.</p>
        <p>Our rugs begin as cotton yarn, dyed in the batch so the colour holds from weave to weave. A weaver then dresses the loom — the warp threads strung to the exact length of the finished rug, itself the first act of precision. From there, the work is rhythm: the shuttle thrown, the beater pulled, the pattern checked row by row. A 4 × 6 ft rug takes roughly two hundred hours across five weeks.</p>
        <p>Because the weft carries the pattern, a dhurrie is reversible by construction — the same on both faces, so it wears on both. Because the weave is flat, it takes less dust, cleans with a vacuum and no beater bar, and folds into a suitcase in a way no tufted rug ever will.</p>
        <p>Look closely at yours and you will find small irregularities: a weft pulled a little tighter on one row, a shade shift where one dye lot ended and the next began. Those are signatures, not defects. Machines remove them by removing everything personal. We leave them in.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-links" style="justify-content:flex-start; margin-top:2.5rem;">
        <a href="/shop?category=rugs">Shop the dhurries</a>
        <a href="/journal">Back to the Journal</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

